 ## Background
-Originating from Bruckner and Gröller's work on Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering [S1], this approach emerged to address the limitations of physical lighting in visualizing complex volumetric structures. It allows artists and scientists to apply non-photorealistic styles (e.g., cartoon shading, matcaps) directly to volume data, enhancing the perception of shape and transparency through illustrative techniques like the 100-percent-rule [S1]. The method is implemented using GPU-based ray casting, leveraging Shader Model 3.0 features for dynamic branching and texture lookups [S1].
+The concept of Style Transfer Functions for Illustrative Volume Rendering was introduced by Stefan Bruckner and Marcus E. Gröller in 2007 [S1]. The method was developed to address the limitations of photorealistic rendering in conveying structural information, drawing inspiration from technical illustrations and the "100-percent-rule" where transparency falls off near edges [S1]. It was implemented using GPU-based ray casting with Shader Model 3.0, integrating seamlessly with existing transfer function pipelines by replacing the color output of the 1D texture lookup with a style index [S1].
 
